description:
Goal: introduce a compact SDK surface that enables sessioned streaming with first-class callback chaining for tool/function calls, and typed adapters that produce structured outputs across TypeScript, Python, and Go.

Why: many existing examples focus on pull-based async iterators or generator resumption. Some customers prefer callback-driven patterns (onData/onTool/onComplete) that better integrate with evented frameworks (React servers, websockets, edge functions). We also need a compatibility shim that maps the new session/callback API to legacy iterator-based code paths for gradual rollout.

Acceptance criteria:
- New SDK surface: SessionStream (TS/PY/Go) exposing start(), resume(token), cancel() and callback hooks: onChunk, onToolResult, onStructured, onComplete, onError.
- Typed tool output adapters: provide a small adapter layer that maps tool invocations to typed objects (JSON schema) and validates/normalizes outputs before calling onStructured.
- Example snippets for TS/Python/Go demonstrating: streaming generation + function/tool call mid-stream + receiving structured output and chaining a follow-up tool invocation using the session token.
- Compatibility shim: back-compat helper that exposes an AsyncIterator over the callback stream and a generator over SDK sessions.
- Tests: unit tests for adapters, integration tests for end-to-end session resume, and perf benchmark showing <2% p99 latency regression for streaming hot path.
- Docs: new page in SDK docs + migration guide + code examples.

Design notes (summary):
- Sessions produce monotonic event envelopes: {type: "chunk"|"tool"|"structured"|"meta", payload, cursor}. SDK exposes typed callbacks and accumulative convenience helpers.
- Tool calls are emitted as events and adapters are applied in the SDK client before the event reaches the user callback (so clients get schema-validated objects).
- Resume tokens are opaque strings from the runtime (cursor sequence + KV checkpoint); SDKs provide resume(token) to rehydrate a session from the server.
- Compatibility shim converts callback events into an async iterator by queueing events internally and yielding them in consumer-facing order.

Proposed TypeScript surface (illustrative):

const session = client.sessionStream({model: 'rwd-7b', prompt: 'Summarize logs:'});

session.onChunk((text) => { /* incremental UI update */ });
session.onToolResult((toolName, raw) => { /* raw tool payload for logs */ });
session.onStructured((schemaType, obj) => { /* already-validated structured output e.g. {severity: 'error'} */ });
session.onComplete((meta) => { /* finalization */ });
session.start();

// To chain: when a structured event arrives, issue a follow-up tool call on the same session so KV cache is reused:
session.onStructured((schemaType, obj) => {
  if (schemaType === 'logSummary') {
    session.callTool('fetch-stack-trace', {requestId: obj.request_id});
  }
});

// Resume example
const token = await session.getResumeToken();
// later or on reconnect
const resumed = client.sessionStream.resume(token);
resumed.onChunk(...); resumed.onComplete(...);

Compatibility shim (TS):

function callbackToAsyncIterator(session) {
  const q = [];
  let done = false;
  const waiters = [];
  session.onChunk((c) => enqueue({type: 'chunk', payload: c}));
  session.onToolResult((n, r) => enqueue({type: 'tool', payload: {n, r}}));
  session.onStructured((t, o) => enqueue({type: 'structured', payload: {t, o}}));
  session.onComplete(() => { done = true; drain(); });
  session.start();

  function enqueue(evt) { q.push(evt); drain(); }
  function drain() { while (q.length && waiters.length) waiters.shift()(q.shift()); }
  return {
    async *[Symbol.asyncIterator]() {
      while (!done || q.length) {
        if (q.length) yield q.shift();
        else await new Promise(r => waiters.push(r));
      }
    }
  }
}

Python sketch (illustrative):

# session = client.session_stream(model='rwd-7b', prompt='Summarize')

def on_chunk(text):
    ui.append(text)

def on_tool(name, raw):
    # adapter will produce typed object if schema known
    pass

def on_structured(schema, obj):
    if schema == 'logSummary':
        # chain a tool on same session (reuses token/kv cache)
        session.call_tool('fetch_trace', {'id': obj['request_id']})

session.on_chunk(on_chunk)
session.on_tool(on_tool)
session.on_structured(on_structured)
session.start()

# Resume
resume_token = session.get_resume_token()
resumed = client.session_stream.resume(resume_token)

# Backcompat helper: convert callbacks to an async generator
async def callback_to_asyncgen(session):
    q = asyncio.Queue()
    done = asyncio.Event()
    session.on_chunk(lambda c: q.put_nowait(('chunk', c)))
    session.on_structured(lambda s, o: q.put_nowait(('structured', s, o)))
    session.on_tool(lambda n, r: q.put_nowait(('tool', n, r)))
    session.on_complete(lambda: done.set())
    session.start()
    while not (done.is_set() and q.empty()):
        yield await q.get()

